`,s.jsx(B,{items:[{q:"Why is detection structurally harder than classification, beyond just being a bigger output?",a:"Because the number of outputs is not fixed. A classifier's output layer has one slot per class and always emits the same shape; a detector must emit a list whose length depends on the image content. Every detector architecture is an answer to that: propose-then-classify, dense-predict-then-suppress, or fixed-slot set prediction with bipartite matching."},{q:"Write the IoU of a (50,40,100,120) ground truth and a (90,70,100,120) prediction, both xywh.",a:"The intersection spans x from 90 to 150 and y from 70 to 160, so 60 x 90 = 5,400. Each box is 12,000, so the union is 12,000 + 12,000 - 5,400 = 18,600. IoU = 5,400 / 18,600 = 0.2903 — a false positive at the usual 0.5 threshold despite looking like a near miss."},{q:"What breaks in a hand-written IoU that forgets max(0, ...) on the intersection dimensions?",a:"Disjoint boxes produce negative width and negative height, whose product is positive, so the function reports a spurious positive overlap for boxes that do not touch at all. Worse, it is sign-correct often enough to pass casual testing and only shows up as inflated mAP."},{q:"Why is IoU preferred over the sum of corner distances as a matching criterion?",a:"Because IoU is scale-free. A 10-pixel corner error is negligible on a 500-pixel object and catastrophic on a 20-pixel one; a distance metric would need a per-scale threshold, while IoU normalises by the boxes' own areas and gives one threshold that works across scales."}]}),`
